WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.680
A dynamic allocation has a lifecycle.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.700
Before allocation, there is no object.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.040
After a successful allocation, there is storage.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.740
After initialization, there may be a valid object according to the program's invariants.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:19.960
Later, ownership may transfer, cleanup may run, and the object becomes invalid for use.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.740
Failed allocations need careful handling.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:29.920
If allocation fails and returns no object, cleanup code should not treat the failed result as an owned initialized object.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:36.860
The surrounding function may still need to release earlier allocations, but it should do so based on tracked state rather than assumptions.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.800
Partially initialized objects are especially important.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:46.100
A structure may have its first member allocated, its second member initialized, and its third member still absent.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.320
Cleanup should release only the members that were successfully initialized and owned.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:56.300
That requires state to be clear enough for the destroy path to distinguish valid fields from untouched fields.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.920
Ownership transfer is another state change.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:06.580
Once a caller transfers an object to a container or another API, the caller should no longer treat the allocation as something it must free.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.720
If the transfer only happens on success, the failure path must say whether the caller still owns the object.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.900
After cleanup, the object is no longer valid.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:20.920
A pointer variable may still contain an address-like value, but the lifetime has ended.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:28.660
Code should not inspect fields, log contents, compare contents, or call another destroy routine on the same allocation after release.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.960
State clarity prevents duplicate cleanup.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:42.860
When the code can identify failed, allocated, initialized, partially initialized, transferred, freed, and invalid states, each branch can make release decisions based on facts rather than habit.
